y  you can't split an oreo evenly in half  well a team of engineers at mit is on  the case they've done extensive research  on why the oreos cream filling seems to  always stick to just one wafer yes it's  a real study published in a real journal  and everything mike drollet digs into  the results with his own sweet study on  how the cookie crumbles  the makers of the oreo cookie have made  it clear over the years if you separate  their cookie do it the right way please  don't fiddle with the oreo  a few years ago there was even a contest  to see if a robot could do it better  apparently that is a big deal for humans  it wasn't exactly a success and then six  months ago engineers at mit decided to  take a run at it with a twist lead  researcher crystal owens wanted to  challenge the oreo way as the only way i  feel like that's a debatable subject my  my preference i feel like the wafer on  its own is a little bit too dry so i  want to have like a little bit of cream  on both sides  they hoped they could make science fun  for kids by engineering the oreometer a  device they created to put the perfect  twist on a cookie alas they failed and  oreo traditionalists let them know  before i did this study i didn't know  those people existed but i feel like  they're just very they're very smug and  satisfied right now now if we were to  accept the mit premise that there needs  to be equal amounts of insides on each  wafer the global new science lab has  come up with a far more efficient method  take one cookie  separate  take a second cookie  separate  take the extra wafers  discard  voila  science  that's too much cream  perhaps but to do it her way wasn't  happening so in her conclusions she  offered the people at oreo a solution  put texture on the inside of the wafers  to grip the cream  they haven't called her back  oh and by the way many many oreos were  harmed during this report mike delay  global news toronto  i think he ate a couple that is global  national for thi